<DocsLayout title="Command line" description="Command reference for the modular Hologram Live CLI.">
  <h1>Command line</h1>
  <p>The CLI is a thin native client. Each top-level command has an independent module under <code>src/cli</code>, while <code>main.rs</code> only initializes observability and delegates.</p>

  <h2>Global options</h2>
  <pre><code>hologram [--config PATH] [--json] [-v|-vv] &lt;COMMAND&gt;</code></pre>
  <div class="table-wrap"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>--config PATH</code></td><td>Use an explicit configuration file.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>--json</code></td><td>Emit a machine-readable JSON result for every command.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>-v</code> / <code>-vv</code></td><td>Increase tracing verbosity for the current process.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p><code>--json</code> is global, so it may appear before or after the subcommand. Successful commands emit one JSON value to stdout—including lifecycle actions, downloads, generated files, accepted mutations, and text-decoded application runs. Runtime failures emit a <code>&#123; "code", "message" &#125;</code> object to stdout and retain a nonzero exit status; diagnostics stay on stderr. Help and completion text remain human-readable.</p>
  <pre><code>hologram status --json | jq -r '.status'
hologram files get blake3:… --output ./asset.bin --json | jq '.byte_length'
hologram run application.holo --output-format text --json | jq -r '.[]'</code></pre>

  <h2>Lifecycle</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap"><table>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>init</code></td><td>Create the default typed configuration.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>serve</code></td><td>Run the service in the foreground.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>start</code></td><td>Start the local service in the background.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>stop</code></td><td>Request graceful shutdown.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>restart</code></td><td>Stop and start the configured local service.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>status</code></td><td>Read health through the configured client route.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>doctor</code></td><td>Validate configuration, module resolution, and local health.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>

  <h2>Capabilities</h2>
  <p>Use <code>modules</code>, <code>registry</code>, <code>files</code>, <code>holo</code>, <code>history</code>, and <code>nodes</code> to address module operations. <code>route</code> explains which configured authority would receive an operation before dispatch.</p>
  <pre><code>hologram modules list
hologram route system.health
hologram route holo.plan
hologram files put ./notes.txt --media-type text/plain
hologram files list
hologram files rename blake3:… meeting-notes.txt
hologram files get blake3:… --output ./notes.txt
hologram registry put ./asset.bin --kind model --media-type application/octet-stream
hologram registry list
hologram history list
hologram nodes list</code></pre>
  <p><code>files</code> is the friendly file-oriented view of the registry. <code>registry</code> lists every object kind, including imported <code>.holo</code> archives. Writes return a BLAKE3 content ID; renaming updates only the display and download name, so that stable ID continues to fetch the exact same bytes.</p>

  <h2>Compile an application</h2>
  <p><code>hologram app init</code> generates a validated source manifest. With a terminal it prompts for ordered layers, kind-specific fields, the primary layer, and an optional capability file. Existing manifests require an explicit <code>--force</code>.</p>
  <pre><code>mkdir my-app
hologram app init my-app

# Non-interactive equivalent for scripts and CI
hologram app init my-app --kind wasm --path app.wasm --entry holo_run</code></pre>
  <p><code>--yes</code> accepts a minimal Wasm manifest using <code>app.wasm</code> and <code>_start</code>. Global <code>--json</code> returns the manifest path, layer count, primary position, and suggested commands as structured output.</p>
  <p><code>hologram compile</code> then packages the JSON application manifest, its capability declaration, and its layer files into a Hologram v4 archive. Readers retain v2/v3 compatibility, and layer paths are relative to the source manifest.</p>
  <pre><code>&#123;
  "schema_version": 1,
  "primary": 0,
  "requires": "capabilities.json",
  "layers": [
    &#123; "kind": "wasm", "path": "app.wasm", "entry": "_start" &#125;,
    &#123; "kind": "view", "path": "ui/index.html", "surface": "portable" &#125;
  ]
&#125;

hologram compile hologram.json --output application.holo
hologram --json holo plan application.holo | jq '.runnable'
hologram run application.holo --input request.bin

# Decode application output when its contract is UTF-8 text or JSON
hologram run application.holo --input request.json --output-format json

# Same manifest identity, payloads resolved through the local κ store
hologram compile hologram.json --thin --output application.thin.holo</code></pre>
  <p>Supported layer kinds are <code>wasm</code>, <code>tensor</code>, <code>rootfs</code>, <code>view</code>, and <code>inference-model</code>. Fat archives are self-contained and can execute directly when their primary layer has a connected provider. A thin archive omits content blobs; import a corresponding fat archive first so resident execution can resolve those layer κ values from the local content cache. Schema v1 assembles prebuilt payloads. Schema v2 also accepts the experimental Python rootfs source recipe described below.</p>
  <p><code>hologram holo plan &lt;PATH|KAPPA&gt;</code> is the read-only preflight command. It verifies identities and required objects, reports layer order, resolution sources, provider availability, limits, and typed blockers, and never includes payload bytes. A local path does not start the service. An unsupported provider produces a successful report with <code>runnable: false</code>, which makes it safe to inspect View, Tensor, rootfs, and inference-model applications in scripts.</p>
  <p><code>hologram run</code> uses binary-safe <code>raw</code> output by default, retaining the execution metadata and byte arrays. <code>--output-format text</code> validates and prints UTF-8 payloads directly, or a JSON string array when global <code>--json</code> is active. <code>--output-format json</code> parses and prints a single JSON result directly, or an array when several <code>--input</code> arguments produce several results. Invalid encodings fail explicitly.</p>

  <h3>Python source projects</h3>
  <p><code>hologram app init --template python --profile rootfs</code> records a project, <code>module:function</code> entrypoint, <code>uv.lock</code>, OCI base, and target architecture in a schema-v2 manifest. <code>hologram compile --check</code> validates the recipe without building it. A normal compile creates an existing <code>rootfs</code> layer—never a new Python layer kind—and requires a running Docker-compatible engine. See <a href={sitePath('/docs/python-apps')}>Python applications</a> for the working NumPy + pandas example and current isolation limits.</p>

  <h3>AI model archives</h3>
  <pre><code>hologram --json ai inspect model.holo

# Low-level assembly of an already-built provider bundle
hologram app init model-app --kind inference-model \
  --path model.bundle --entry ai.default --engine uor-r4
hologram compile model-app/hologram.json --output model-app.holo</code></pre>
  <p><code>ai inspect</code> reads service entry, engine, content identity, and embedded size without initializing an inference engine. The normal model-source compiler remains <code>hologram-ai</code>, which produces a complete v4 archive. Live's compile manifest accepts a prebuilt opaque provider bundle for low-level composition; it does not duplicate model compilation or validate a private R4 bundle itself. See <a href={sitePath('/docs/model-apps')}>AI model applications</a>.</p>

  <h2>Operations</h2>
  <p><code>tracing</code> reads or updates the live filter, <code>openapi</code> writes the generated OpenAPI document, and <code>update</code> checks, installs, or rolls back verified releases.</p>
</DocsLayout>
